    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "Clinical manifestations have been categorized as classic SPS or variant SPS, such as stiff limb syndrome and progressive encephalomyelitis. The stiffness and rigidity in classic SPS can be triggered by minimal physical or emotional stimuli, over time causing increased anxiety and severe disability. In SPS, antibodies in the CSF commonly target the GAD antigen on gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) neurons. However, anti-GAD antibodies are present only in two-thirds of patients. GABA neurons inhibit spontaneous discharges from spinal motor neurons. Therefore, their absence leads to a continuous activation of muscle motor units.",
      "quote": "Clinical manifestations have been categorized as classic SPS or variant SPS, such as stiff limb syndrome and progressive encephalomyelitis[2, 6, 9]. Thestiffnessandrigidityinclassic SPS can be triggered by minimal physical or emotional stimuli, overtime causing increased anxiety and severe disability [6]. In SPS, antibodies in the CSF commonly target the GAD antigen on gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) neurons[1]. However, anti-GAD antibodies are present only in two-thirds of patients [6, 9]. GABA neurons inhibit spontaneous discharges from spinal motor neurons [1]. Therefore, their absence leads to a continuous activation of muscle motor units.",
      "confidence": "high"
